{"p":"/environment/atmosphere","t":"Atmospheric effects","d":"Atmospheric effects simulate realistic environments by scattering sunlight in unique ways.","h":"<p><strong>Atmospheric effects</strong> simulate realistic environments by scattering sunlight\nin unique ways based on properties that control air particles. Using the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere\"><code>Atmosphere</code></a> object, you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Control particle density.</li>\n<li>Create a silhouette or blend distant objects with an offset.</li>\n<li>Simulate a haze or glare.</li>\n<li>Set an atmosphere's color or decay.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Atmospheric effects used to render a brilliant sunset scene\" /></p>\n<h2>Atmosphere properties</h2>\n<p>Every property of the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere\"><code>Atmosphere</code></a> object inside the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Lighting\"><code>Lighting</code></a> service works together to support the overall vision, themes, and mood of your game.</p>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Atmosphere object shown in Explorer window of Studio\" /></p>\n<h3>Density</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Density\"><code>Density</code></a> property controls how many particles exist in the air of your game; the higher the density, the more particles, which obstruct a player's view of objects and terrain.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"0\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Density-A.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Density value of 0\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"0.35\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Density-B.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Density value of 0.35\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"callout c-info\">\n<p>Density only <strong>directly</strong> affects objects and terrain; however, since you view a <a href=\"#/docs/environment/skybox\">skybox</a> through those objects and terrain, the visibility of a skybox is also affected.</p>\n</div>\n<h3>Offset</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Offset\"><code>Offset</code></a> property controls how light transmits between the camera and the sky background. When you increase this value, it creates a horizon silhouette; when you decrease this value, it blends distant objects into the sky for a seemingly endless and seamless open world.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"0\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Offset-A.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Offset value of 0\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"1\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Offset-B.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Offset value of 1\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"callout c-warning\">\n<p>Carefully balance the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Offset\"><code>Offset</code></a> property with the <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Density\"><code>Density</code></a> property. A low offset value may cause the <a href=\"#/docs/environment/skybox\">skybox</a> to be seen through objects and terrain, and a high offset value may cause distant objects and terrain to have too much detail for your desired lighting effect.</p>\n</div>\n<h3>Haze</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Haze\"><code>Haze</code></a> property controls the haziness of the atmosphere to create a visible effect both above the horizon and far into the distance from the camera. To create environmental moods, like a smoky tint for a polluted alien planet or a foggy blue tint for a somber game, combine this property with the <a href=\"#color\">Color</a> property.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"1\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Haze-A.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Haze value of 1\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"2.8\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Haze-B.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Haze value of 2.8\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n<h3>Color</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Color\"><code>Color</code></a>\nproperty sets the hue of the atmosphere for subtle environmental moods\nand themes. To expand this property's visible effect, combine it with a high\n<a href=\"#haze\">Haze</a> property value.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"[255, 255, 255]\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Color-A.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Color value of [255, 255, 255]\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"[255, 200, 255]\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Color-B.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Color value of [255, 200, 255]\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n<h3>Glare</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Glare\"><code>Glare</code></a>\nproperty sets an atmospheric glare around the sun. A high value results in an\nincreased effect of sunlight cast onto the sky and game. To see this property's visible effect, you <strong>must</strong> combine a glare with a\n<a href=\"#haze\">Haze</a> value higher than 0.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"0\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Glare-A.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Glare value of 0\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"1\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Glare-B.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Glare value of 1\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>\n<h3>Decay</h3>\n<p>The <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/Atmosphere/Atmosphere.Decay\"><code>Decay</code></a>\nproperty sets the hue of the atmosphere away from the sun, gradually falling off\nfrom <a href=\"#color\">Color</a> toward this value. To see this property's visible effect, you <strong>must</strong> combine a glare with <a href=\"#haze\">Haze</a> and <a href=\"#glare\">Glare</a> values higher than 0.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\">\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"[255, 255, 255]\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Decay-A.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Decay value of [255, 255, 255]\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n<div class=\"tab\" data-label=\"[255, 90, 80]\">\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"../assets/lighting-and-effects/atmosphere/Decay-B.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Atmosphere with Decay value of [255, 90, 80]\" /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n</div>\n</div>","kid":{"simple":"The `Atmosphere` object, placed inside `Lighting`, makes the air in your world look real. It controls how much stuff is floating in the air, how hazy the distance looks, the color of the sky glow, and the glare around the sun. With a few numbers you can make a clear sunny day, a foggy swamp, or a smoky alien planet.","analogy":"Atmosphere is like the air on a real day. On a clear day you can see mountains far away. On a misty morning, far-off trees fade into gray, and at sunset the whole sky glows orange around the sun.","keyPoints":["Put an `Atmosphere` object inside the `Lighting` service to use these effects.","`Density` sets how many particles are in the air. Higher values hide far-away objects and terrain more.","`Offset` controls how light travels between the camera and the sky. Higher values make a clear horizon outline; lower values blend far objects into the sky.","Balance `Offset` and `Density`. Too low an offset can let the skybox show through objects.","`Haze` adds a hazy look above the horizon and in the distance. Pair it with `Color` for moods like a smoky or foggy tint.","`Glare` adds a glow around the sun, but only shows if `Haze` is above 0.","`Decay` is the sky color away from the sun, fading from `Color`. It only shows when both `Haze` and `Glare` are above 0."],"terms":{"atmosphere":"The air in your game world and how it affects light and how far you can see.","particles":"Tiny bits like dust or water drops floating in the air. More particles make it harder to see far.","density":"How packed something is. Atmosphere `Density` 0 is perfectly clear; 0.35 is noticeably hazy.","offset":"Here it means how much the far distance blends into the sky. 0 blends far objects into the sky; 1 gives a sharper horizon outline.","silhouette":"A dark outline shape against a brighter background, like mountains against a sunset.","horizon":"The line far away where the ground seems to meet the sky.","haze":"A soft, misty look in the distance.","glare":"The bright glow around the sun.","decay":"The color the sky fades toward as you look away from the sun.","hue":"A color tint, like pink or blue, given as RGB numbers such as [255, 200, 255].","skybox":"The big background image of the sky surrounding your world."},"example":{"title":"Switch the weather to foggy","place":"Script in ServerScriptService","code":"local Lighting = game:GetService(\"Lighting\")\n\n-- Find the Atmosphere, or make one if it does not exist\nlocal atmosphere = Lighting:FindFirstChildOfClass(\"Atmosphere\")\nif not atmosphere then\n\tatmosphere = Instance.new(\"Atmosphere\")\n\tatmosphere.Parent = Lighting\nend\n\n-- Clear day\natmosphere.Density = 0.3\natmosphere.Haze = 0\natmosphere.Glare = 0\n\ntask.wait(10)\n\n-- Foggy and gray-blue\natmosphere.Density = 0.6\natmosphere.Haze = 2.8\natmosphere.Color = Color3.fromRGB(180, 200, 220)","explain":["The script finds the Atmosphere inside Lighting, or creates one.","First it sets a fairly clear day with low density and no haze or glare.","After 10 seconds it raises `Density` and `Haze` so far-off things fade away.","The gray-blue `Color` makes the fog feel cold and gloomy."]},"watchOut":["Turning up `Glare` with `Haze` at 0. You will see nothing; raise `Haze` first.","Setting `Decay` without `Haze` and `Glare` above 0. It has no visible effect.","Putting the Atmosphere somewhere other than inside `Lighting`.","Using a very low `Offset` with low `Density`. The sky can show through your terrain and buildings."]}}